==English==
{{wikipedia|Trooper (rank)}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|troop|er}}, from {{der|en|fr|troupe}}. The sense of “one who endures adversity” comes from {{m|en|trouper||member of an acting troupe}} but through assimilation with the sense of “soldier” has come to be usually spelled “trooper”.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹuːpə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-trooper.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːpə(ɹ)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|military}} A soldier of private rank in [[cavalry]] or [[armour]]. {{defdate|from 1640}}
#: {{syn|en|cavalryman|horse soldier|crewman|armored soldier|zipperhead|q5=Canadian military slang|Tpr|q6=abbreviation}}
# A [[cavalry]] horse; [[charger]].
# A [[soldier]].
# {{lb|en|British}} A [[troopship]].
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[state trooper]]. {{defdate|from 1911}}
# {{lb|en|Australia}} A [[mounted]] [[policeman]]. {{defdate|from 1858}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|colloquial}} One who endures [[adversity]] or [[hardship]] with an attitude of [[stoicism]] and [[persistence]]. {{defdate|from 1959}}
#: {{syn|en|survivor|tough cookie}}
#: {{ux|en|He was a real '''trooper''' about taking care of the kids for the weekend.}}
#* '''2005''', Justin Watral, ''Firehouse 101'' (page 272)
#*: Because his father and brothers were in the department, he had to be. She knew he was never really comfortable with the job but still he did it like a '''trooper'''.

